<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Perche</h1><p class="st-meta">1079 CE – 1226 CE (approx.) · capital Mortagne-au-Perche</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A hilly county between Normandy and Maine whose crusading counts fought in Spain and the Holy Land before it lapsed to the French crown.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mortagne-au-perche">Mortagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogent-le-rotrou">Nogent</a> gathered the forested hill country of the Perche in the 11th century, and around 1079 Geoffrey II was styled count of Perche. The county lay on the frontier between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a> and the royal domain, and its counts balanced between the Anglo-Norman kings and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetians</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Perche was a land of forests, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>, later famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percheron">Percheron</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotrou-iii-count-of-perche">Rotrou III</a> fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and in Aragon's wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, and married a daughter of Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Count Thomas was killed fighting for the French prince Louis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a> in 1217.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last count, William, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons</a>, died in 1226, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX</a> absorbed the county into the royal domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>. It was later granted as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to royal princes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-perche.jpg" alt="County of Perche" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_ville_fr_Chambellay_(Maine-et-Loire).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1079 CE – 1226 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mortagne-au-Perche</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rotrou III of Perche, Thomas of Perche, William of Perche</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Perche</dd></dl></aside></div>
